    "updated_at": "2026-08-03T12:04:05Z",
    "body": "See [Firefox bug 2003514](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2003514).\n\nI believe the spec was mostly written based on web-observable behavior. The load event for initial `about:blank` in auxiliary browsing contexts, `DOMContentLoaded`, and `readyState` are hard to test from the web.\n\nThe spec currently fires load for initial `about:blank` in [#iframe-load-event-steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#iframe-load-event-steps) only for iframes. `readyState` is initialized as `complete` according to [#current-document-readiness](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#current-document-readiness) and doesn't change.\n\nGecko creates the document eagerly in `nsDocShell::CreateInitialDocumentViewer` with `readyState` being `complete` and when the load for `about:blank` arrives, we short-circuit it in `nsDocShell::CompleteInitialAboutBlankLoad` to be synchronous. This fires `load` and `DOMContentLoaded` independent of whether it loads in an iframe or new window. We keep `readyState` set to `complete` throughout.\n\nI'm unsure what Blink does implementation wise, but the webdriver tests in above bug shows that they change `readyState`, fire `DOMContentLoaded`, and fire events for new windows.",
